title: Following references
tags: Query
kind: reference
aliases: follows traversal arrow
summary: -> follows a ref or a tree_ref to its referent; ->* and =>* reach every descendant in a tree, strictly or with the target.

|!Form |!Matches |
|`field -> (query)` |metarecords whose `field` points at a metarecord matching `query` — a `ref`'s target, a `tree_ref`'s parent |
|`field -> "path"` |`tree_ref`: metarecords whose parent is the node at `path` |
|`field ->* "path"` / `field ->* (query)` |`tree_ref`: every descendant of the node at `path`, or of any node matching `query` — the nodes themselves excluded |
|`field =>* "path"` / `field =>* (query)` |the same with the nodes themselves: the whole subtree |

```
author -> (name = "Coltrane")
mfr_path -> (mfr_path:value = "2021")     # the direct children of every folder named 2021
mfr_path ->* (mfr_path:value = "2021")    # everything inside them
tag -> (path =>* "music/jazz")            # a tag and all its narrower tags
tag -> (3f2a…9b)                           # the records pointing at that metarecord
```

`->*` and `=>*` are `tree_ref`-only: on a `ref` field they are refused with `400`.
A path operand follows [[TreeRef path conventions]]; one that is no node matches
nothing. A sub-query target is evaluated on its own, and overlapping subtrees count
once. In the IR the arrows are `follows` and `follows_transitive` (`inclusive: true`
for `=>*`).

A subtree is one read of the node's descendant bitmap, so `mfr_path ->* "/music"`
costs the same on a folder of ten files and on one of a million
([[The forest in the store]]).

The strict default of `->*` is the opposite of `same`'s reflexive one
([[Same-value matching]]): there, including the target is a special case of a
traversal; here, excluding it would break the class.
